===Etymology===
Latinized translation of {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|Bohème|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{term|Boiohaemum|lang=la}}, compound of Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*haimaz||home}} (more at {{l/en|home}}) and ''[[w:Boii|Boio]]''- ‘the Boii’, the Celtic tribe previously inhabiting the area. Bohemia was abandoned by the Boii ''ca''. 60 BCE. and settled by the Germanic [[w:Marcomanni|Marcomanni]] shortly thereafter.<ref>Dietz, Karlheinz (Würzburg). “Boiohaemum.” ''Brill’s New Pauly'', 2012. Reference. 14 March 2012 <http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/brill-s-new-pauly/boiohaemum-e218860></ref> Related to {{l/en|Bavaria}}.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{context|lang=en|geography}} {{altname|Czechia|lang=en}}, the [[land]]s of the [[Czech Republic]].
# {{context|lang=en|geography|historical}} The [[medieval]] [[kingdom]] of the [[Czech]] [[lands]], [[both]] as an [[independent]] [[state]] and as a [[region]] and [[title]] of the [[Austria]]n [[empire|Empire]].
#* '''1759''', George Sale et al., ''The Modern Part of an Universal History'', {{nowrap|volume XXIX}}: ''History of the German Empire'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=dFtjAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA2 page&nbsp;2]:
#*: Since the reign of ''[[Charlemagne]]'', this country is divided into ''[[High Germany|High]]'' and ''[[Low Germany]]'': the [[first|firſt]], towards the [[south|ſouth]], comprehending the ''[[Palatinate]]'' of the ''[[Rhine]]'', ''[[Franconia]]'', ''Suabia'', ''[[Bavaria]]'', '''''[[Bohemia]]''''', ''[[Moravia]]'', ''[[Austria|Auſtria]]'', ''[[Carinthia]]'', ''[[Carniola]]'', ''[[Stiria]]'', ''[[Tyrol]]'', the ''[[Swiss|Swiſs]]'', and the ''[[Grisons|Griſons]]''...

====Usage note====
In historical use, Bohemia is sometimes noted as a region of "[[Germany]]" but this was due to now-obsolete uses of that name to describe the Holy Roman or Austrian empires. Since the unification of the German states by Prussia in 1871, Bohemia has no longer been considered "German", although its [[Sudeten]] region was contested during the [[World Wars]].

